{"type":"message","id":"26d1179e","parentId":"6da91b4b","timestamp":"2026-10-17T22:49:55.466Z","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"I wonder if the test ran before or after the edit since they were in the same block, but since it passed with 3 tests, it must have run after the edit was applied, since test_dates would've failed otherwise.\n\n","thinkingSignature":""},{"type":"text","text":"Fixed: `parse_date` in `dates/parse.py` now uses `datetime.fromisoformat(s)` instead of `strptime` with a fixed format. All 3 tests in `tests/test_parse.py` pass.\n\n**Cause:** the format `\"%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%S\"` had nothing to match the trailing `Z` (the UTC marker), so `strptime` raised.\n\n**Behaviour changes to know about:**\n- Timestamps ending in `Z` now come back timezone-aware (UTC). Timestamps without a zone still come back naive, as before.\n- Code that compares or subtracts results from both kinds of input will raise `TypeError` (Python won't mix naive and aware datetimes). `day_key` doesn't do that, so it's unaffected.\n- `parse_date` now accepts more input than before: offsets like `+02:00`, fractional seconds, and a bare date. All of these are valid ISO-8601, which is what the function's docstring says it parses.\n- Reading `Z` this way needs Python 3.11 or newer. This environment runs 3.13.5. The repo has no config file declaring a minimum Python version, so I couldn't check whether older versions need support."}],"api":"anthropic-messages","provider":"vertex-anthropic","model":"claude-opus-5-5","usage":{"input":2,"output":485,"cacheRead":25194,"cacheWrite":1783,"totalTokens":27464,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0},"cttl":{"ephemeral5m":1783}},"stopReason":"stop","timestamp":1792277389587,"responseId":"msg_vrtx_011Cg8dHLuLVNeZ7MLu27iAB","duration":5876.237971000002,"ttft":952.7389320000002,"contextSnapshot":{"promptTokens":26979,"nonMessageTokens":21589,"compactionEpoch":0},"errorId":0}}
